   "# Contract Comparison Report — microFIT Contract Version 2.0 vs Version 3.1.1\n\nPrepared: Comparison of two provided contract texts (Version 2.0 — Document A; Version 3.1.1 — Document B)\n\n## Executive overview\nThe two contracts are substantially identical in structure, scope and most substantive provisions (term, pricing, environmental attributes, metering, access rights, etc.). Key differences are limited and localized to a few provisions that change the Program's information-collection/disclosure rules and remove explicit domestic content warranty/requirement references. There are also minor drafting changes that formalize notification procedures.\n\nSummary of the most material changes:\n- Removal of explicit references to a Minimum Required Domestic Content Level (60%) and the Supplier warranty regarding Domestic Content. (Material — reduces Supplier obligations tied to domestic content.)\n- Expanded description of permitted information disclosures (adds explicit authority to disclose Facility details to other government entities for public health, safety and emergency planning). (Material — broader data-sharing/privacy exposure.)\n- Formalization of notification procedure: Supplier must notify \"in the Prescribed Form\" for certain updates (operational impact — procedural).\n- Minor renumbering of subclauses where deletions occurred.\n\n## Section-by-section findings (high level)\n- Identical / functionally identical: Title and general recitals, Part 1 – Contract Details (majority of fields), Appendix lists, Part 2 Sections 1 (Term), 2 (Pricing and Payment), 3 (Environmental Attributes), most of Section 4 (Representations) and Section 5 (Covenants), Section 6 (General Conditions) are the same in substance.\n- Notable differences (see detailed section diffs below): Part 1.B(5)(iv) [Minimum Required Domestic Content Level], Part 2 Section 4 (Representations and Warranties — removal of domestic content warranty), Part 2 Section 5.1.2 (notification \"in the Prescribed Form\"), Part 2 Section 6.5 (Collection of Information / Disclosure), and related minor renumbering.\n\n## Practical implications and recommended actions\n- If you are a Supplier: the removal of the explicit domestic content warranty may relieve a prior obligation, but confirm whether other Program rules or external laws still impose domestic-content requirements. Also, the expanded disclosure language increases the scope of information that may be shared; consider updating privacy/internal consent processes and informing any representatives.\n- If you are OPA/LDC: the changes align the contract with broader data-sharing expectations (including emergency planning). Ensure internal processes for \"Prescribed Form\" notices and approvals are in place and that data-sharing safeguards comply with FIPPA and internal privacy policy.\n- For both parties: note clause renumbering and confirm references in ancillary documents (Connection Agreement, application forms, internal processes) remain consistent. Consider an appendix or redline cross-reference indicating where obligations were removed/added.\n\n## Recommended next steps\n1. Legal review to confirm whether the removal of domestic content language was intended to change eligibility/price entitlements or simply to update the agreement to reflect program-level rule changes.\n2. Privacy and compliance review to document the new scope of disclosures and the retention/handling of shared data.\n3. Operationalize the \"Prescribed Form\" and notify Suppliers of the required format for notices.\n4. Update any templates and ancillary agreements to reflect clause renumbering and changed wording.\n\nFor full per-section detail and an itemized list of key differences and their likely impacts, see the section diffs and key-differences below.",
